  "notes": "Earliest published comparative lexical wordlist of the Tangkhul language and the archaic Champhung variety collected by Capt. George Gordon and published in 1837 by Nathan Brown in JASB. Out of copyright.",
  "alternate_title": "Comparison of Indo-Chinese Languages",
  "background": "Published in 1837 in the Journal of the Asiatic Society of Bengal (Vol. 6, pp. 1023-1038) by American Baptist missionary and scholar Nathan Brown. The lexical elicitations were collected by Captain George Gordon, Political Agent in Manipur, and document two distinct varieties: the northern Luhuppa (Tangkhul) and the archaic Champhung language. This is the earliest known published comparative wordlist for any Tangkhulic speech variety, predating McCulloch by 22 years.",
  "phonology_notes": "Transcribed using early 19th-century British orientalist Roman orthography. Preserves archaic Champhung initial clusters and distinct lexical cognates before extensive 20th-century levelling.",
